³3minor³30³3newauthor³31³2username³2Junker³2host³2pD9F78FC2.dip.t-dialin.net³2revision³28³2tscreate³21095793744³2id³21210³2keepts³21102267172³1version³21³2ts³21102267172³2ip³2217.247.143.194³2name³2text_default³2data³2summary³3*³3text³3<b>041205, Junker:</b> I started gulching.org with NFS a few weeks ago. I like NFS. I like the cheap and clear business and billing model. I like their control panels. I like their attitude. It might be that they get too expensive if you have a *very* active site, but otherwise, I say that they're the ones to go with. As my other sites run their two year contracts out, I'll switch them to nfs or the next generation equivalent.

Cost:
~$0.01/month Data Transfers (rate 1GB/$1.00)
~$0.03/month Disk Space (rate $0.01/MB/month)
~$0.30/month Basic Domain Name service (rate: $3.65/year)
~$0.58/month Domain Name registration (outsourced rate: ~$7.00/yr)

Total: ~$0.92/month or $11.02/year

Compared, Acenet costs $4.00/month ($48.00/year). But that's for standard 2GB space & 6GB transfer/month, but not including domain name registration of $15/year.

The breakeven point is 400MB disk size or 4GB transfer. My largest site to date is 35MB and negligable transfer. It's cost then at NFS would be $1.24/month ($14.88/year). The only remaining advantage to Acenet is its bundle of automatically installable software-- forums, wikis, blogs, etc. You have to load the software by hand at NFS.

- - -

